 ## Background
-NeRF was introduced by Mildenhall et al. in 2020 [S1]. It established the paradigm of using continuous volumetric functions parameterized by neural networks for novel view synthesis. The method gained widespread adoption due to its ability to synthesize photorealistic images from unstructured image collections without requiring explicit 3D geometry or camera calibration beyond initial estimates. It has since become the foundational reference for numerous extensions in dynamic scene rendering, real-time optimization, and generative 3D modeling.
+The concept of Neural Radiance Fields was introduced by Mildenhall et al. in 2020 [S1]. The method implicitly represents a radiance field using an MLP, computing pixel color by accumulating samples along camera rays via volume rendering [S1]. The MLP takes a point $p$ as input and outputs density $\sigma$ and color $c$ [S1]. The field is trained with a reconstruction loss between the reconstructed color and ground-truth color $C_{gt}(\mathbf{r})$, i.e., $\mathcal{L}_{rec} = \sum_{\mathbf{r} \in \mathcal{R}} \| \hat{C}(\mathbf{r}) - C_{gt}(\mathbf{r}) \|_2^2$ [S1].
 
